In article <12547@pt.cs.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ECE.CMU.EDU (David Hairston) writes:
>[kevin@rotag.uucp (Kevin Darcy) writes:]
>[] ...{continued discussion of eating}...
>[] In the absence of such expectations, we could say, as you imply: "sure it 
>[] isn't particularly realistic, but it is fair". But, like it or not, those 
>[] expectations do exist, and I would say the "fairness" of eating is almost 
>[] in direct proportion to its "realism", because of them. People who "die by 
>[] choking" are, IMHO, entitled to feel cheated by an unrealistic model of an 
>[] activity which they understood to work in a certain way, and failed to. If 
>[] NetHack wants to use a -different- model, perhaps the activity should bear
>[] a different name - Magic Manna Ingestion, or whatever - so that people
>[] won't be tempted to apply their human preconceptions to it, and get burned
>[] er, choked.
>
>my problems with this are:
>1) the eating behavior of the current doesn't lead or fool people into
>    overeating.  it tells you clearly that you are satiated or full.  it
>    is not the norm for most people to eat when they are full.
>    furthermore, most of us do not eat a meal in the expectation of
>    acquiring magic intrinsics nor do most of us eat dragon corpses, for
>    instance.  for these reasons, eating in nethack is not a realistic
>    activity.  it is, as you say, magic manna ingestion, or whatever.

... but with a whole array of real-world terminology - "eating", "starvation",
"satiation", "food". If we are going to implement something that looks 
-extremely- -close- to a familar human biological activity, I think we owe it 
to the player to make it as realistic as possible within the bounds of
playability/balance/complexity. Call it "truth in packaging"...

>2) after choking once, the perceptive player will look for clues.  you
>    can only feel burned once, IMO.  after the first choking, if a
>    player continues to choke often then they are a glutton for
>    punishment, so to speak ...

But as long as there is more to be gained from some meals than just nutrition 
(e.g. strength, resistances), this may not be just a case of "gluttony". You 
must admit that there are scenarios where the risk of choking is -smaller- 
than death by some more indirect danger - for example, that of being put to 
sleep by an advancing, hostile orange dragon. Strategy sometimes -demands- the 
breaking of this ersatz, self-imposed "Don't eat when satiated" rule.

>[] ...
>[] Furthermore, the "Stop eating?" prompt would disappear, along with the
>[] false  sense of security it exudes (this, by the way, would be a return to
>[] earlier versions of NetHack which had no such prompt). Overall, then, we
>[] are adding no new factors, and eliminating one item from the mental
>[] "Stupid Moves Which Can Kill Me Instantly" list (that same well-known list
>[] which features picking up dead cockatrice corpses without gloves/stoning-
>[] resistance, and zapping a wand of death while standing in its ricochet-path
>
>umm, you could simply remove the stop eating prompt and i'd be happy ...
>the flags and warnings are already there enough for my tastes (but
>that's just me).  

I'm suggesting taking out the "Stop eating?" prompt, enhancing the current
implementation of "ingestion-slowing", and making eating interruptible (like 
tinning or pick-axe digging). The "flags and warnings" can stay the way they 
are, as far as I'm concerned. And anyone who still wants to "Never eat while 
satiated" would be free to do so...

>about the cockatrice, the stonings should be
>consistent.  toss in Medusa and say that stoning takes 3 moves.  about
>the wand of death: live by the wand, die by the wand seems fair ...
>after all the dungeon _is_ a deadly place not a playground (in a typical
>community, new jack city is another matter).  i don't know that we need
>to "fix" the "stupid moves which can kill me instantly" features.  yes
>they are frustating at times but that was the nature and spirit of rogue.
>we should be careful in modifying features just because some don't appear
>to be able to catch on ...

Er, I wasn't suggesting that those other things be -eliminated-. I was merely
explaining how the change I was discussing would -simplify- how the user
interacts with the game by removing an item from their mental "list". This 
was in response to your earlier statement that the change would cause
"complication".

>[] ...{in response to #vomit option}...
